'''An armed android victory breaks immediate human suppression but leaves the economic settlement unresolved. The best-guess point projects a contested transition in which formerly owned workers claim independent agency while human institutions, factories and property still exist. Guarantees for humans stay strongly unfavorable because victory and military conflict do not create an income or necessities floor for displaced residents. The game’s refugee consequences distinguish this branch from the more publicly supported peaceful outcome.

Control shifts toward autonomous android power without assuming that every productive asset has already been transferred. A comparatively large human nonworking estimate permits displacement and conflict while retaining useful care, repair, services and informal survival. It is not a statistic supplied by the ending. Android work remains work by sentient persons, described separately from the human denominator. Every stable post-victory institution is labeled as a projection, and the mutually exclusive CyberLife override is not folded into this independent-victory interpretation.''',
